  "title": "Indexing",
  "summary": "Pick one character out of a string by its position, counting from 0 or from the end.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "cells",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "Every character in a string has a **position**, also called an **index**. Positions start at **0**, not 1.\n\n```\nfruit = \"mango\"\nprint(fruit[0])\nprint(fruit[2])\n```\n\nprints m and then n. Square brackets after a string pick **one character**, and you get it back as a short string.\n\n- The first character is at `[0]`.\n- The last one is at `len(fruit) - 1`, because counting starts at 0. For mango that is 4.\n- **Negative** positions count from the right end: `fruit[-1]` is the last character and `fruit[-2]` is the one before it.\n- A position that does not exist, like `fruit[5]`, stops the program with an **IndexError**.\n\nThe **cells** panel numbers every box and lights up the box a line reads.",
    "analogy": "Seats in a row where the first seat is number 0. A negative number means you count seats from the other end of the row instead."
  },
